  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is herd immunity?",
      "a": "When enough people in a community are immune to a germ, it can't spread easily, so people who aren't immune are protected too. It's also called community immunity.",
      "asks": [
        "What does herd immunity mean?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a germ spread from person to person?",
      "a": "Many germs spread in tiny droplets when people cough, sneeze or breathe, or on hands and surfaces. Each sick person can pass the germ on to others.",
      "asks": [
        "How do germs spread?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Who can't have some vaccines?",
      "a": "Very young babies before they reach the age for a vaccine, and some people whose immune systems are weakened by illness or treatment. Herd immunity helps protect them.",
      "asks": [
        "Why can't everyone be vaccinated?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How many people need to be immune for herd immunity?",
      "a": "It depends on how catchy the germ is. For measles, one of the catchiest, about 95 percent of people need to be immune.",
      "asks": [
        "What percent is needed for herd immunity?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do vaccines protect a person?",
      "a": "A vaccine shows the immune system a harmless piece or weakened form of a germ, so it learns to recognise it and can fight the real germ quickly later.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a vaccine work?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "For measles, about 95% of a population needs to be immune for herd immunity.",
    "Herd immunity helps protect people who cannot be vaccinated, such as very young babies."
  ],
